{
 "id": "food_FOOD00058",
 "name": "Coffee (food medium)",
 "category": "food",
 "organism_scope": "food microbiome / general",
 "aerobic": true,
 "description": "Food-derived medium: metabolizable components of Coffee (Coffea) [Coffee and coffee products] from FooDB, plus a standard M9 mineral base. Organic components use presence-based uptake bounds; FooDB content retained. Measured compounds with no BiGG exchange are listed under uncovered.",
 "namespace": "bigg",
 "provenance": {
  "source_type": "database",
  "citation": "FooDB v2020-04-07 (Wishart DS et al., foodb.ca). Content sources: DTU, PHENOL EXPLORER, USDA.",
  "doi": "",
  "url": "https://foodb.ca/downloads",
  "notes": "Organic components mapped from FooDB compounds to BiGG via InChIKey/name. Mineral base added. Bounds presence-based; foodb_content retained. Unmapped measured compounds kept in uncovered (v2 completeness fix). IMPORTANT: composition values are taken from the archived FooDB 2020-04-07 CSV data release; FooDB's live food page no longer displays this bulk composition, so it will appear empty. Verify individual compounds via the per-component cross-reference links. Underlying content was aggregated by FooDB from USDA / DTU / Phenol Explorer.",
  "data_release": "FooDB 2020-04-07 CSV release (foodb.ca/downloads)",
  "food_record": "https://foodb.ca/foods/FOOD00058",
  "verification": "expert-curated (multi-source food composition; growth-relevant profile completed, FooDB noise removed)",
  "curation_date": "2026-07-17",
  "curation_sources": [
   "USDA FoodData Central / SR Legacy NDB 14209 'Coffee, brewed from grounds, prepared with tap water'",
   "Rune, Giacalone, Steen et al. 2023, Current Research in Food Science 6:100485, doi:10.1016/j.crfs.2023.100485 (individual organic acids & sugars in French-press brewed coffee)",
   "Konieczka et al. 2020, LWT (S0023643820303339) and Wu et al. 2023 (trigonelline in coffee brews)",
   "FooDB FOOD00058 (Coffee)"
  ],
  "curation_notes": "For a growth medium, brewed coffee is a dilute (~99% water), nitrogen-poor beverage whose dominant carbon/energy substrates are ORGANIC ACIDS — chlorogenic and quinic acids first, then acetic, citric, formic, lactic, malic — with only modest free glucose/fructose that survive roasting (light roasts only). Caffeine (~40 mg/100 mL) and trigonelline (~50 mg/100 mL) are abundant but largely non-nutritive/inhibitory. Potassium is the main mineral. Acid/sugar amounts come from a specialty French-press",
  "curation_added": [
   "chlorogenic acid",
   "quinic acid",
   "citric acid",
   "acetic acid",
   "phosphoric acid",
   "lactic acid",
   "formic acid",
   "malic acid",
   "glycolic acid",
   "D-glucose (with amount)",
   "D-fructose (with amount)",
   "caffeine (with amount)",
   "trigonelline (with amount)"
  ],
  "curation_removed": [
   {
    "name": "Cholesterol",
    "reason": "Animal sterol; biologically implausible in a plant-derived beverage; FooDB predicted, not measured."
   },
   {
    "name": "Retinol",
    "reason": "Preformed (animal-form) vitamin A; USDA reports 0 in brewed coffee; implausible in a plant beverage."
   },
   {
    "name": "Beta-Carotene",
    "reason": "USDA reports negligible/zero provitamin A carotenoid in brewed coffee; FooDB predicted."
   },
   {
    "name": "Cyanocobalamin",
    "reason": "Vitamin B12 is not synthesised by plants; not present in coffee; FooDB predicted/implausible."
   },
   {
    "name": "Phytomenadione",
    "reason": "Vitamin K1; USDA reports 0 in brewed coffee; negligible in the brew."
   },
   {
    "name": "L-Ascorbate",
    "reason": "Vitamin C; USDA reports 0 in brewed coffee; heat/roast-labile and absent from the beverage."
   },
   {
    "name": "Starch C12H20O10",
    "reason": "No starch is extracted into the brewed beverage (retained in the grounds); USDA carbohydrate ~0."
   },
   {
    "name": "Nickel",
    "reason": "Ultratrace, non-nutritive in this matrix; FooDB contaminant-level entry, not a growth nutrient."
   },
   {
    "name": "Co2+",
    "reason": "Cobalt ion at ultratrace/contaminant level; not a defined growth nutrient of the brew."
   },
   {
    "name": "Decanoic acid / Dodecanoic acid / Pentadecanoic acid / Octanoic acid / Heptanoate / Hexanoate / alpha-Linolenic acid / (Z,Z)-9,12-octadecadienoic acid / C18:1 n-9",
    "reason": "Filtered brewed coffee contains ~0.02 g/100 g total lipid; these green-bean fatty acids are almost entirely retained in the grounds/paper filter and are not meaningful growth substrates in the brew."
   }
  ],
  "curation_confidence": "medium",
